course: dictionary
lesson: 9043
title: Slay: an old word with a new meaning
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one the writer means. The word slay is a good example. Its old meaning, found in fairy tales and the Bible, is to kill violently: knights slay dragons, and David slew Goliath. Slew is the past tense and slain is the form used after words like was.
explain: Today, children use slay in a happy way. «You slayed that dance!» means you did it brilliantly. Nobody was hurt. The word dance is the clue, and there is no danger anywhere in the sentence, so the old meaning does not fit.
explain: Slay can also mean making someone laugh a lot: «That joke slays me.» Try the swap check: if you put in the old meaning, the joke would have killed someone, which makes no sense. The new meaning fits, so that is the one to use.
fact: «Slay» means to kill violently, especially in battle or in a story.
fact: In slang, «slay» means to do something brilliantly or look amazing, as in «You slayed that dance!»
fact: «Slay» can also mean to make someone laugh a lot, as in «That joke slays me.»
fact: The past forms depend on the meaning: slew and slain are used for the old meaning, while slayed is the slang past form.
fact: Clues to the meaning: a dragon, a battle or a sword point to the old meaning, while a dance, a performance or a laugh points to the new one.
exercise: In the old stories, «slay the dragon» means what? | To make someone laugh | To hide from danger | To dance brilliantly | *To kill violently
exercise: Maya's friend says, «You slayed that dance!» What does she mean? | You were late for the dance | You danced in the rain | *Your dance was brilliant | You hurt the dance
exercise: Ali says, «That joke slays me.» What does he mean? | The joke was about a dragon | The joke was too long to hear | The joke made him very sad | *The joke made him laugh a lot
test: In an old tale, the hero had to slay the monster. What does slay mean here? | To paint carefully | To sing loudly | To sleep deeply | *To kill violently
test: Fill the gap: The dragon was ___ by the brave knight. | slaying | slays | slid | *slain
exam: Which sentence uses slay to mean do something brilliantly? | *Her solo slayed everyone at the concert. | The knight rode out to slay the dragon. | The soldiers fought to slay the enemy in battle.
